AU  - Li, Mengze
AU  - Wang, Tianbao
AU  - Zhang, Haoyu
AU  - Zhang, Shengyu
AU  - Zhao, Zhou
AU  - Miao, Jiaxu
AU  - Zhang, Wenqiao
AU  - Tan, Wenming
AU  - Wang, Jin
AU  - Wang, Peng
AU  - Pu, Shiliang
AU  - Wu, Fei
PY  - 2022
AB  - Natural language spatial video grounding aims to detect the relevant objects in video frames with descriptive sentences as the query. In spite of the great advances, most existing methods rely on dense video frame annotations, which require a tremendous amount of human effort. To achieve effective grounding under a limited annotation budget, we investigate one-shot video grounding and learn to ground natural language in all video frames with solely one frame labeled, in an end-to-end manner. One major challenge of end-to-end one-shot video grounding is the existence of videos frames that are either irrelevant to the language query or the labeled frame. Another challenge relates to the limited supervision, which might result in ineffective representation learning. To address these challenges, we designed an end-to-end model via Information Tree for One-Shot video grounding (IT-OS). Its key module, the information tree, can eliminate the interference of irrelevant frames based on branch search and branch cropping techniques. In addition, several self-supervised tasks are proposed based on the information tree to improve the representation learning under insufficient labeling. Experiments on the benchmark dataset demonstrate the effectiveness of our model.
